    private static int[] merge(int[] arr1, int[] arr2) {
        int i = 0, j = 0, k = 0;
        int[] mergedArr = new int[arr1.length + arr2.length];

        while (i < arr1.length && j < arr2.length) {
            if (arr1[i] <= arr2[j]) {
                mergedArr[k++] = arr1[i++];
            } else {
                mergedArr[k++] = arr2[j++];
            }
        }

        while (i < arr1.length) {
            mergedArr[k++] = arr1[i++];
        }

        while (j < arr2.length) {
            mergedArr[k++] = arr2[j++];
        }

        return mergedArr;
    }
}
```

**Explanation:**

* The `merge()` method takes two sorted arrays as input and returns a new sorted array containing the elements of both arrays.
* It uses a two-pointer approach to compare the elements of the two arrays.
* If the elements from the first array are smaller or equal to the elements from the second array, they are added to the merged array. Otherwise, the elements from the second array are added.
* Once the loop completes, any remaining elements from either array are appended to the merged array.
* Finally, the method returns the sorted merged array.

**Time Complexity:** O(n), where n is the total length of the two arrays.

**Space Complexity:** O(n), due to the creation of the new merged array.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class MergeSort {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.println("Enter the size of the first array: ");
        int size1 = scanner.nextInt();

        int[] arr1 = new int[size1];

        System.out.println("Enter the elements of the first array: ");
        for (int i = 0; i < size1; i++) {
            arr1[i] = scanner.nextInt();
        }

        System.out.println("Enter the size of the second array: ");
        int size2 = scanner.nextInt();

        int[] arr2 = new int[size2];

        System.out.println("Enter the elements of the second array: ");
        for (int i = 0; i < size2; i++) {
            arr2[i] = scanner.nextInt();
        }

        int[] mergedArr = mergeSort(arr1, arr2);

        System.out.println("Merged and sorted array: ");
        for (int element : mergedArr) {
            System.out.print(element + " ");
        }
    }

    private static int[] mergeSort(int[] arr1, int[] arr2) {
        int[] mergedArr = new int[arr1.length + arr2.length];

        int i = 0, j = 0, k = 0;

        while (i < arr1.length && j < arr2.length) {
            if (arr1[i] <= arr2[j]) {
                mergedArr[k++] = arr1[i++];
            } else {
                mergedArr[k++] = arr2[j++];
            }
        }

        while (i < arr1.length) {
            mergedArr[k++] = arr1[i++];
        }

        while (j < arr2.length) {
            mergedArr[k++] = arr2[j++];
        }

        return mergedArr;
    }
}
```

**Explanation:**

* The `mergeSort` method takes two sorted arrays as input and returns a new sorted array that contains the elements of both arrays.
* The method first creates a new array to hold the merged results.
* It then iterates through both arrays and compares the elements.
* If the element from the first array is less than or equal to the element from the second array, it is added to the merged array. Otherwise, the element from the second array is added.
* Once both arrays have been iterated through, the remaining elements from either array are added to the merged array.
* Finally, the merged array is sorted and returned.

