```java
public class RadixSort {

    public static void radixSort(int[] arr) {
        int maxNum = arr[0];
        for (int i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
            if (arr[i] > maxNum) {
                maxNum = arr[i];
            }
        }

        int exp = 1;
        while (maxNum / exp > 0) {
            countingSort(arr, exp);
            exp *= 10; // Move to the next digit
        }
    }

    private static void countingSort(int[] arr, int exp) {
        int[] count = new int[10]; // Count array to store the count of each digit
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            int index = arr[i] / exp % 10;
            count[index] = count[index] + 1;
        }

        for (int i = 1; i < count.length; i++) {
            count[i] += count[i - 1];
        }

        int[] temp = new int[arr.length]; // Temporary array to store the sorted numbers

        for (int i = arr.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            int index = arr[i] / exp % 10;
            temp[count[index] - 1] = arr[i];
            count[index] = count[index] - 1;
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            arr[i] = temp[i];
        }
    }
}
```

**Explanation:**

* **Counting Sort:** This is a non-comparative sorting algorithm that counts the number of occurrences of each digit in the input array.
* **Radix Sort:** This is an extension of counting sort that sorts numbers in multiple passes, each pass focusing on a different digit.
* **Negative Numbers:** To handle negative numbers, we can represent them as positive numbers by adding a large constant to them.
* **Efficiency:** The time and space complexity of the algorithm are O(kn), where k is the number of digits in the maximum number.

**Example:**

```java
int[] arr = {170, 45, 75, 90, 802, 24, 2, 66};
radixSort(arr);
```

**Output:**

```
[2, 24, 45, 66, 75, 90, 170, 802]
```

