```javascript
function validatePhoneNumber(phoneNumber) {
  const regex = /^\(?([0-9]{3})\)?[- ]?([0-9]{3})[- ]?([0-9]{4})$/;
  return regex.test(phoneNumber);
}
```

**Explanation:**

* **^**: Matches the beginning of the string, ensuring the entire input is a phone number.
* **(?([0-9]{3})\)?**: Matches the area code (optional), which can be preceded by a parentheses and up to 3 digits.
* **[- ]?**: Matches either a hyphen or a space character, allowing for either formatting.
* **([0-9]{3})**: Matches the exchange code, which consists of 3 digits.
* **[- ]?**: Matches either a hyphen or a space character again.
* **([0-9]{4})**: Matches the line number, which consists of 4 digits.
* **$**: Matches the end of the string, ensuring the entire input matches the pattern.

**Usage:**

```javascript
const phoneNumber1 = "555-123-4567";
const phoneNumber2 = "(555) 123-4567";
const invalidPhoneNumber = "555-123-456";

console.log(validatePhoneNumber(phoneNumber1)); // true
console.log(validatePhoneNumber(phoneNumber2)); // true
console.log(validatePhoneNumber(invalidPhoneNumber)); // false
```

