Please write a function that takes an HTML string as input and returns a JSON object representing the parsed HTML.

Additional requirements:
1. The function should handle nested HTML tags.
2. The function should handle attributes of HTML tags and include them in the JSON object.
3. The function should handle multiple occurrences of the same tag and store them as an array in the JSON object.

Example input:
```html
<html>
<body>
<h1 class="heading">Hello World!</h1>
<div>
  <p>This is a paragraph.</p>
  <p>This is another paragraph.</p>
</div>
</body>
</html>
```

Expected output:
```json
{
  "html": {
    "body": {
      "h1": {
        "_attributes": {
          "class": "heading"
        },
        "_value": "Hello World!"
      },
      "div": {
        "p": [
          "This is a paragraph.",
          "This is another paragraph."
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Note: The attribute key-value pairs are stored in a separate "_attributes" object for each tag, and the tag content is stored in the "_value" field. Multiple occurrences of the same tag are stored as an array.

response:
```javascript
function parseHtml(htmlString) {
  const result = {};
  let currentElement = null;
  let attributes = [];

  htmlString = htmlString.trim();
  htmlString = htmlString.replace(/<\/?[a-z]+>/gi, '</$1>'); // Remove self-closing tags

  for (let char of htmlString) {
    if (char === '<') {
      const tagName = htmlString.substring(htmlString.indexOf('<') + 1, htmlString.indexOf('>'));
      if (currentElement) {
        result[currentElement]['_attributes'] = attributes;
        attributes = [];
      }
      currentElement = tagName;
    } else if (char === '>') {
      result[currentElement]['_value'] = htmlString.substring(htmlString.indexOf('>') + 1);
      currentElement = null;
    } else if (currentElement) {
      attributes.push({ [char]: htmlString.substring(htmlString.indexOf(' ') + 1, htmlString.indexOf('=')) });
    }
  }

  if (currentElement) {
    result[currentElement]['_attributes'] = attributes;
  }

  return { html: result };
}
```

**Example Usage:**

```javascript
const htmlString = `
<html>
<body>
<h1 class="heading">Hello World!</h1>
<div>
  <p>This is a paragraph.</p>
  <p>This is another paragraph.</p>
</div>
</body>
</html>
`;

const parsedHtml = parseHtml(htmlString);

console.log(parsedHtml);
```

