## Nested For Loop in C++

**Purpose:**

Nested for loops are used to iterate over two or more sets of data simultaneously, performing complex operations on the elements. 

**Example:**

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < rows; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < cols; j++) {
        matrix[i][j] = input[i] * weights[j];
    }
}
```

**Explanation:**

* The outer loop iterates over rows of a matrix.
* The inner loop iterates over columns of the matrix.
* For each element in the matrix, the value is calculated by multiplying the element of the input array with the element of the weights array.


**Erroneous Code:**

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < rows; i++) {
    for (int j = rows; j < cols; j++) { // Error: index out of bounds
        matrix[i][j] = input[i] * weights[j];
    }
}
```

**Error:**

The inner loop index `j` is incorrectly initialized to `rows` and iterates from `rows` to `cols` range, resulting in an index out of bounds exception.

**Key Points:**

* Nested loops can be used for complex operations involving multiple sets of data.
* Ensure loop indices are within the bounds of the array being iterated.
* Check for potential errors in loop bounds to avoid runtime exceptions.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class AcademicSubjectSorting {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] academicSubjects = {"Mathematics", "Natural Science", "English Literature", "Historical Studies", "Mathematics", "Natural Science"};

        // Sort the array using our custom sorting algorithm
        sort(academicSubjects);

        // Read input from the user
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.println("Enter the name of an academic subject: ");
        String subjectName = scanner.nextLine();

        // Check if the subject is included in the sorted array
        boolean isFound = binarySearch(academicSubjects, subjectName);

        // Print a message indicating whether the subject is included in the sorted array
        if (isFound) {
            System.out.println(subjectName + " is included in the sorted array.");
        } else {
            System.out.println(subjectName + " is not included in the sorted array.");
        }

        // Count the frequency of the input subject
        int frequency = countFrequency(academicSubjects, subjectName);

        // Print the times or usage of the input subject
        System.out.println("The frequency of " + subjectName + " is " + frequency);
    }

    private static void sort(String[] arr) {
        // Custom sorting algorithm implementation
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < arr.length; j++) {
                if (arr[i].compareTo(arr[j]) > 0) {
                    String temp = arr[i];
                    arr[i] = arr[j];
                    arr[j] = temp;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    private static boolean binarySearch(String[] arr, String key) {
        int low = 0;
        int high = arr.length - 1;

        while (low <= high) {
            int mid = (low + high) / 2;

            if (arr[mid].equalsIgnoreCase(key)) {
                return true;
            } else if (arr[mid].compareTo(key) < 0) {
                low = mid + 1;
            } else {
                high = mid - 1;
            }
        }

        return false;
    }

    private static int countFrequency(String[] arr, String key) {
        int count = 0;

        for (String subject : arr) {
            if (subject.equalsIgnoreCase(key)) {
                count++;
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
}
```

**Output:**

```
Enter the name of an academic subject: Mathematics
Mathematics is included in the sorted array.
The frequency of Mathematics is 2
```

