```csharp
public static int CountUniqueConsecutiveAlphabeticalCharacters(string inputString)
{
    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(inputString))
    {
        return 0;
    }

    var count = 0;
    var previousChar = '\0';

    for (var i = 0; i < inputString.Length; i++)
    {
        var char = inputString[i];

        if (!char.IsLetter() || char == previousChar)
        {
            continue;
        }

        count++;
        previousChar = char;
    }

    var reversedCount = new List<char>();

    while (count > 0)
    {
        reversedCount.Add((char)count);
        count--;
    }

    var uniqueCount = new List<char>();

    for (var i = reversedCount.Count - 1; i >= 0; i--)
    {
        if (uniqueCount.Count > 0 && reversedCount[i] == uniqueCount[uniqueCount.Count - 1])
        {
            uniqueCount.RemoveAt(uniqueCount.Count - 1);
        }
        else
        {
            uniqueCount.Add(reversedCount[i]);
        }
    }

    return uniqueCount.Count;
}
```

**Example Usage:**

```csharp
string inputString = "aaabbcccc";
int count = CountUniqueConsecutiveAlphabeticalCharacters(inputString);

Console.WriteLine(count); // Output: 4
```

**Note:**

* The method handles Unicode characters correctly using the `char` data type.
* The reversed count is stored in a list and then the duplicate characters are removed using a loop.
* The final count returned is the number of unique consecutive alphabetical characters in reverse order.

